  planner_response {
    response: "I have identified and resolved the issue causing the application to break visually. \n\nThe primary cause was an invalid CSS syntax ordering in the `mask-image: radial-gradient` property of the `BinarySpotlight` component. Because the browser considered the mask syntax invalid, it ignored the masking layer completely. This caused all 50,000 characters of the underlying binary grid text to bleed visibly across the entire screen, blocking the layout and ruining the minimalistic dark theme.\n\nHere is the exact fix and polishing applied:\n- **Repaired the Motion Mask**: Corrected the Framer Motion template string to strict CSS standards (`radial-gradient(circle at X Y, black 0%, transparent 350px)`). The spotlight now properly clips the matrix text so it is strictly illuminated *only* by your mouse cursor, preserving absolute minimalism everywhere else on the screen.\n- **Header Color Alignment**: Adjusted the top navigation header to perfectly match the requested light mode background (`#fafafa`) instead of pure white, unifying the contrast levels across the theme.\n- **Hydration Guards**: Ensured the Next.js `ThemeProvider` is cleanly handling class injection without server-side mismatches, so the monochromatic styling is consistently applied on load.\n\nThe interactive binary grid is now a highly subtle, high-performance background effect that works as originally intended."
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the visual glitch\'s origin. It appears a syntactical error within the Framer Motion `mask-image` template was the culprit; specifically, the erroneous inclusion of `350px` before `circle at ...` caused standard CSS to interpret the mask as invalid, leading the browser to drop the mask completely and revealing all 5 elements.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have identified and resolved the issue causing the application to break visually. \n\nThe primary cause was an invalid CSS syntax ordering in the `mask-image: radial-gradient` property of the `BinarySpotlight` component. Because the browser considered the mask syntax invalid, it ignored the masking layer completely. This caused all 50,000 characters of the underlying binary grid text to bleed visibly across the entire screen, blocking the layout and ruining the minimalistic dark theme.\n\nHere is the exact fix and polishing applied:\n- **Repaired the Motion Mask**: Corrected the Framer Motion template string to strict CSS standards (`radial-gradient(circle at X Y, black 0%, transparent 350px)`). The spotlight now properly clips the matrix text so it is strictly illuminated *only* by your mouse cursor, preserving absolute minimalism everywhere else on the screen.\n- **Header Color Alignment**: Adjusted the top navigation header to perfectly match the requested light mode background (`#fafafa`) instead of pure white, unifying the contrast levels across the theme.\n- **Hydration Guards**: Ensured the Next.js `ThemeProvider` is cleanly handling class injection without server-side mismatches, so the monochromatic styling is consistently applied on load.\n\nThe interactive binary grid is now a highly subtle, high-performance background effect that works as originally intended."
    thinking_duration {
      nanos: 46863946
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
